<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murzuq</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Murzuk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murzuq is an oasis town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. From the 16th century it was the capital of the Awlad Muhammad sultans of Fezzan and a major station on the caravan route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> region. Its large mud-brick fort dates from the Ottoman period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murzuq.jpg" alt="Murzuq" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franzfoto · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murzuq_-_Moschee_und_Festung_Qala_at_Turk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
